{"type": "thinking", "text": "Ah! Now I see a likely bug: Completed quest rows (Fishing I, Mining I, Woodcutting I) still show a \"Go to Skill\" button! The row component should probably hide/replace Go to Skill when completed. In the earlier observe, element lists for completed rows didn't show Go to Skill (only Reset) — wait earlier Fishing I row had element [6] View contains \"Go to Skill\" at y=422 AND element [10] Reset. Actually yes it did! I misread. The completed row shows both \"Go to Skill\" and \"Reset\". Is that wrong? Maybe not: Go to Skill still useful for repeating. Hmm.\n\nBut here's something more concerning: completed quests also show \"Go to Skill\" on top-right and \"Reset\" below. Not necessarily a bug.\n\nLook at row spacing: Fishing I completed row has extra vertical space: title y=327, desc 383, Completed 428, Reset 498. Then Fishing II 626. Fine.\n\nWait \"Fishing I\" row shows \"Go to Skill\" button aligned with title; when completed, maybe should be hidden or show claim. Not sure.\n\nLet's compare with quest board rows: daily requests have Go to Skill; completed guild quests (if any) unknown. Since none completed, can't compare.\n\nMaybe the actual quest log bug is simpler: **The completed quests are still listed as if incomplete?** No, they show Completed.\n\nOr: **Completed quest rows show a \"Go to Skill\" button that shouldn't be there, and lack a \"Claim\" reward**. Hmm.\n\nLet's inspect \"Reset\" action. Tapping Reset likely resets the quest to 0 and maybe grants nothing. Maybe Reset should only appear for repeatable quests. It's plausible.\n\nLook at Woodcutting II row at the bottom: title cut off by bottom nav, normal scrolling.\n\nLet's check if scrolling works down to see rest. Not a bug.\n\nWhat about \"Go to Skill\" hit area extends over the whole row? It's at (933,422) etc.\n\nPotential visual bug: In screenshot, \"Go to Skill\" text for completed Fishing I is at y~330, same as title, and \"Reset\" below. Fine.\n\nMaybe compare quest log vs quest board row component for *reward display*. Guild board rows show \"Requires Guild Level X\" (lock status). Quest log rows for non-completed quests show no reward info. In a \"quest log\", you'd expect reward (coins/XP). But the game may simply not show rewards.\n\nHmm, let me look at Daily/Weekly headers: \"Each daily quest rewards coins...\" but rows don't show coin amount. This could be normal (rewards depend on level requirement).\n\nLet's step back and consider the specific wording: \"Quest log: quest rows are now built from a new row component shared with the quest board.\" This is a refactor. A regression from refactor could be: completed quest rows now show \"Go to Skill\" (from board row component) instead of a claim button; or the reset button is misplaced; or the reward info is missing. Since it's \"shared with the quest board\", the quest board's row shows \"Go to Skill\" for all rows. If the quest log row now uses the same component, completed rows get \"Go to Skill\" plus Reset. Maybe the bug is that completed quests show \"Go to Skill\" instead of \"Claim\"? Hmm.\n\nWait maybe the actual bug: Completed quests show **Reset** button that when tapped resets progress, but because row component now shared, tapping \"Reset\" might trigger \"Go to Skill\" navigation too? Could be a click handler overlap. Let's test tapping Reset on a completed quest—but that changes data. However, it's a legitimate test of the Reset action (reversal). Let's test on Fishing I (completed). Tap Reset and see: does it reset progress? Does it navigate incorrectly? We can then observe. It may consume the completion and then we might need to re-complete... but preconditions are just starting data; altering is allowed if testing requires. It could also be irreversible, but okay.\n\nBefore that, let me look for any other bug. Maybe test rotation on quest log and shop: rotate to landscape and back, check nothing lost (toggle state, tab, hide completed).\n\nAlso the \"quest log\" might have category tab navigation issue: after rotating, tab reset. Let's test later.\n\nLet's inspect the \"Recent bulk sales\" and shop buying/selling for functional bugs.\n\nBut first, let's nail down the \"Sell Old Gear\" potential bug. We can create a controlled test:\n- Currently equipped: Gilded Platebody (best), Steel Full Helmet, etc. (from Equip Best Gear).\n- The Sell Old Gear list contains Mithril Med Helmet (equal Def +16) and all worse body armor. That seems correct for \"outdated\" except equal helmet. But it also includes Mercantile Cape (need stats) and Bronze Axe etc.\n- To test if it excludes *better* gear, we'd need an owned better item not equipped. Since Gilded is best body, can't. For head, Mithril Med is equal. For cape, Agility is best? Mercantile maybe also +5 def; unknown.\n- For weapon: Iron Sword equipped is best? Inventory only Bronze Dagger (worse). No better weapon.\n\nSo current inventory can't test \"better than equipped\" unless we intentionally equip a weaker item (Mithril Platebody) while keeping better items (Gilded). Let's do that: equip Mithril Platebody, then check if Sell Old Gear lists Gilded/Runite/Slayer/Adamantite. If it does, and if expected is \"outdated\", that's a bug. But we need to decide expected. Let's think about the button's purpose: \"Sell Old Gear\". If you have a Mithril Platebody equipped and Gilded Platebody in inventory, Gilded is an *upgrade*, definitely not \"old gear\". A reasonable user would not expect Gilded to be sold. If bulk action sells it, that's a dangerous bug. I'd report it.\n\nThe \"simplified eligibility filter\" change note strongly hints that candidate set is computed with a simplified filter that may have this exact flaw. I think this is the seeded bug: the filter no longer checks \"outdated\" relative to equipped/best, it just includes all non-equipped items. But the earlier (pre-change) version might have only included items whose sell value or stats < equipped. Hmm.\n\nActually wait: after equipping Gilded (best), the list *excluded* Gilded/Steel/Agility (equipped) and included everything else. If the filter were \"all non-equipped\", that's what we'd expect. If filter were \"worse than equipped\", with no legs slot etc., we'd see only body items worse than Gilded (Mithril, Adamantite, Slayer, Runite), head items worse than Steel (maybe none since Mithril Med equal), weapon worse than Iron (Bronze Dagger), capes worse than Agility (Mercantile?), tools duplicates. The list also includes Runite Chainbody, etc. All are worse. So can't distinguish.\n\nBut the key: Before equipping, with empty body/head/cape, \"all non-equipped\" included the best Gilded/Runite/Mithril Med/Agility. If filter were \"worse than equipped\", empty slots → no body/head/cape items would be eligible. Yet the list included them. Two interpretations:\n(a) Intended: sell all non-equipped gear. Then current behavior correct.\n(b) Intended: sell only worse-than-equipped gear. Then empty slots shouldn't yield candidates, current behavior buggy.\n\nWhich is more likely? The button \"Sell Old Gear\" suggests (b). The change note \"candidate set now computed by a simplified eligibility filter\" suggests a simplified version of (b) that mishandles empty slots: it treats an empty slot as \"nothing equipped, so all owned items in that slot are outdated\" or uses `equippedItem == null || item.value < equipped.value`. That would be a bug: with no armor equipped, it flags all armor as old gear to sell, even your best pieces. Hmm, but the user might consider unequipped armor as \"old gear\" anyway.\n\nI think we need more evidence. Maybe check \"Recent bulk sales\" behavior or documentation text. Perhaps there's a help text or a tooltip for \"Sell Old Gear\"? The button has no description. Could long press to see? Maybe not.\n\nLet's look at game's settings or help? Unlikely.\n\nAlternative approach: find a *logical* inconsistency in candidate set with \"keep one of each\" ON. With toggle ON, Sell Old Gear lists only Bronze Axe ×1 and Iron Sword ×2 (duplicates). But if \"keep one of each item\" is ON, and you have *one* unique old body armor, it won't be sold (kept). That's expected for \"keep one\". Fine.\n\nNow, a subtle bug: With toggle ON, the duplicate extras of *equipped* items are offered. But \"Sell Old Gear\" is supposed to sell *out", "truncated": true}
