{"type": "thinking", "text": "Now equipped Mithril Platebody (Def 48), and Sell Old Gear includes:\n- Adamantite Platebody (Def 66) — BETTER than equipped!\n- Runite Platebody (Def 90) — BETTER\n- Slayer Platebody (Def 80) — BETTER\n- Runite Chainbody (Def 60) — BETTER\n- Gilded Platebody (Def 95) — BETTER, but wait Gilded is missing from this list!? We equipped Mithril; Gilded should be in inventory. The list shows Adamantite, Runite Chain, Runite Plate, Slayer, but **not Gilded Platebody**! Hmm! That's strange. Did we somehow sell Gilded? No. Maybe the list is scrolled and Gilded is below? There are 9 items visible + maybe hidden. Let's compute total 4,217: 937+25+25+62+6+437+1250+1250+225 = 4,217 exactly. So no hidden items. Gilded missing entirely.\n\nWait, maybe when we equipped Mithril, the Gilded Platebody was... no, it should be inventory x1. Let's check Profile inventory to see Gilded count. But why is Gilded missing from Sell Old Gear? Perhaps because **the \"simplified eligibility filter\" considers Gilded Platebody as ineligible because it's the highest-tier/best item** (protect best gear), while it includes other better-than-equipped items. Hmm! Interesting.\n\nAlternatively maybe Gilded was still *equipped in another loadout*? No.\n\nLet's inspect inventory now: Profile → Inventory shows Gilded? Let's check. Earlier inventory list after Equip Best Gear didn't show Gilded because it was equipped. Now it should appear. Let me check quickly. But first note this asymmetry: With Mithril equipped, Sell Old Gear lists better items (Adamantite, Slayer, Runite Plate, Runite Chain) but excludes Gilded (the best). Why would it exclude only Gilded? Maybe because Gilded is **equipped in the \"Best Gear\" loadout** or flagged as best. Or maybe because its sell price/value comparison uses an \"outdated\" definition: item is outdated if its sell value <= some threshold? Gilded sells 300, Runite 1250, Adamantite 937, Slayer 225, Chain 1250. Not threshold.\n\nMaybe \"Gilded Platebody\" is excluded because it's *locked* or *favorited*? Precondition says no item locked. Hmm. Or because it has a \"unique\" flag? Gilded/Gilded may be special gear (from Divine?). Maybe Gilded is considered best-in-slot by the filter and protected. But then why were Runite Platebody etc. included when Gilded was equipped? They were included because worse than Gilded. When Mithril equipped, Gilded (better) is excluded, but other better items are included. That suggests the filter compares each item to the *equipped* item but has a direction bug for some? Wait if filter is \"item.value < equipped.value\" then when Mithril (48) equipped, only worse items should be included: Mithril? Hmm none of the listed are worse except maybe none. All listed better. So direction is wrong? Or filter is \"item is not equipped and not best\"? Hmm.\n\nLet's enumerate with Mithril equipped (Def 48):\n- Adamantite 66 included\n- Runite Chainbody 60 included\n- Runite Platebody 90 included\n- Slayer 80 included\n- Gilded 95 excluded\n- Mithril Platebody equipped (excluded)\n- Other armor: Steel Full Helmet equipped (Def16) excluded; Mithril Med Helmet (Def16) included; Agility Cape equipped; Mercantile Cape included; Bronze Boots equipped.\n\nSo the only armor excluded besides equipped is Gilded (the highest defense). Everything else not equipped included, including strictly better ones. That suggests the filter's rule is: include all non-equipped items, EXCEPT the \"best\" item in each slot? But then Runite Platebody (Def 90) is not best because Gilded (95) is; included. Mithril Med Helmet equal to equipped Steel (16), but maybe Steel is \"best\" by tie-break, so Mithril included.\n\nSo the rule might be \"include all items that are not the best item for their slot\" (i.e., \"outdated\" = not best-in-slot), regardless of what's equipped. Then when Gilded was equipped, Runite (second best) included, etc. And when Mithril equipped, Gilded is best-in-slot so excluded (protected), while everything else included including better-than-equipped. This is the **simplified eligibility filter**: candidate = not best in slot (and not equipped). That makes sense! The correct behavior for \"Sell Old Gear\" would be probably candidate = worse than equipped. The simplified version incorrectly uses \"best owned\" rather than \"equipped\"? Hmm, maybe \"old gear\" means \"not your best gear\", which is a sensible bulk action: sell all gear that isn't part of your best loadout. Then equipping Mithril doesn't matter; the best item is protected. That could be intended: \"Sell Old Gear\" = sell everything except your best gear per slot. Then candidate set is correct! In that case, the feature is fine, and our scenario is expected: it sells all gear except the best (Gilded) and equipped/worn. That's actually a useful bulk action and consistent with \"keep best\".\n\nHmm, but then the list name \"old gear\" fits: anything not best = old. But it included Bronze Dagger vs Iron Sword (best), Mercantile Cape vs Agility Cape (best), Mithril Med Helmet (equal to Steel Full but not \"best\" due tie), etc. So yes: candidate = all equipment not part of \"best gear\" loadout. The \"simplified eligibility filter\" likely computes exactly that. So no bug there? Hmm.\n\nBut wait, then why did it include extra copies of equipped items (Bronze Axe x1, Iron Sword x2)? Because those are duplicates beyond the best/equipped copy. Under \"sell all non-best gear\", duplicates are extra, so included. Reasonable.\n\nSo Sell Old Gear might be correct after all. Ugh.\n\nLet's check if Gilded is in inventory now to confirm exclusion is best-in-slot protection. Profile Inventory. But it's likely.\n\nOK let's stop chasing this. We need to find actual defects. Maybe run through all interactions and look for anomalies. Let's examine the toggle restore behavior more rigorously, including the specific \"when the shop opens\" semantics: maybe the toggle save is not updated if you leave via tab switch? We tested leave via Home and reopen, it persisted on. But maybe if you toggle and then close the app (kill) immediately, it doesn't save? We can't force-stop easily but can press Back to launcher and reopen; earlier persisted.\n\nWait, there might be a subtle bug: The \"Keep one of each item\" toggle state is stored in save data, but the *bulk action computation* uses a non-restored local default. We need to test: set toggle ON, leave shop, reopen, then open Sell Junk *first thing* before toggling. If quantities are not reduced, bug. Let's test this cleanly:\n1. Currently Sell Old Gear sheet open and toggle off (sheet). Cancel.\n2. Toggle ON.\n3. Exit shop to home (back arrow then wait).\n4. Reopen shop, go Sell.\n5. Immediately open Sell Junk and check quantities (expect -1).\nLet's do it.\n\nCancel sheet (element 32)."}
